---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME } from '../consts';
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="What CalcBadger is, how the calculators are verified, and how to get in touch."
>
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-[680px]">
		<h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)]">About CalcBadger</h1>

		<div class="mt-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.85] text-[var(--ink)]/75">
			<p class="mb-4">
				CalcBadger is a collection of free online calculators built around one rule: <strong
					>show the work</strong
				>. Every tool states the formula it uses, names the standard or source the formula comes
				from, and walks through an example you can check by hand. If a calculation has competing
				conventions (tax years, building codes, test curves), the page says which one it follows.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-4">
				Each calculator's math lives in a small, isolated function with unit tests whose expected
				values were worked out from authoritative sources (government tables, published standards,
				textbook formulas) rather than from the code itself. When a tool depends on data that
				changes annually, the page is dated and reviewed on that cycle.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-4">
				Everything runs in your browser. There are no accounts, and nothing you type into a
				calculator is transmitted or stored; the numbers never leave the page.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-4">
				The site is built and maintained by {AUTHOR_NAME}. For corrections, suggestions, or
				anything else: <a
					href="mailto:contact@calcbadger.com"
					class="text-[var(--flag-dark)] underline underline-offset-2">contact@calcbadger.com</a
				>. Found a number that looks wrong? Please say so. Accuracy reports get fixed first.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>
